explain.narrative:
## Core concept
FULL OUTER JOIN preserves all rows from both inputs. Matching pairs are combined; an unmatched left row has NULLs for right-side columns, and an unmatched right row has NULLs for left-side columns. Multiple matches produce multiple paired rows.

## SQLite syntax
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
SELECT a.columns, b.columns\nFROM table_a AS a\nFULL OUTER JOIN table_b AS b ON a.key_column = b.key_column;
```

## Sample schema and data
The examples use a small customers table and an orders table. customer_id is the relationship key. The sample deliberately includes two customers without orders and one order whose customer_id has no matching customer. This lets you observe both matching and unmatched rows.

## Worked examples
### 1. Create and inspect both inputs
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER, order_total NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha Rao','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal Khan','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra Das','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev Patel','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,50),(104,9,40);
SELECT (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers) AS customers, (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders) AS orders;
```
Expected result: 4 | 4

The sample includes customers without orders and an order without a customer.

### 2. Preserve rows from both tables
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER, order_total NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha Rao','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal Khan','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra Das','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev Patel','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,50),(104,9,40);
SELECT c.customer_name, o.order_id FROM customers AS c FULL OUTER JOIN orders AS o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id ORDER BY COALESCE(c.customer_id, o.customer_id), o.order_id;
```
Expected result: Asha Rao | 101; Asha Rao | 102; Bilal Khan | NULL; Chitra Das | 103; Dev Patel | NULL; NULL | 104

The result contains matched pairs plus unmatched customers and unmatched orders.

### 3. Find only unmatched rows from either side
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER, order_total NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha Rao','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal Khan','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra Das','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev Patel','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,50),(104,9,40);
SELECT c.customer_name, o.order_id FROM customers AS c FULL OUTER JOIN orders AS o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id WHERE c.customer_id IS NULL OR o.order_id IS NULL ORDER BY COALESCE(c.customer_id, o.customer_id);
```
Expected result: Bilal Khan | NULL; Dev Patel | NULL; NULL | 104

The NULL test on each table's non-nullable primary key identifies which side lacks a partner.

### 4. Reconcile row counts by match status
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER, order_total NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha Rao','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal Khan','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra Das','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev Patel','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,50),(104,9,40);
SELECT CASE WHEN c.customer_id IS NOT NULL AND o.order_id IS NOT NULL THEN 'matched' WHEN c.customer_id IS NOT NULL THEN 'customer_only' ELSE 'order_only' END AS match_status, COUNT(*) AS rows_in_group FROM customers AS c FULL OUTER JOIN orders AS o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id GROUP BY match_status ORDER BY match_status;
```
Expected result: customer_only | 2; matched | 3; order_only | 1

The CASE expression classifies each result row into matched and unmatched groups.

### 5. Compare full join with a left join
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER, order_total NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha Rao','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal Khan','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra Das','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev Patel','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,50),(104,9,40);
SELECT c.customer_name, o.order_id FROM customers AS c FULL OUTER JOIN orders AS o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id WHERE c.customer_id IS NULL ORDER BY o.order_id;
```
Expected result: NULL | 104

A LEFT JOIN cannot preserve right-only rows; the full join can. SQLite added RIGHT and FULL OUTER JOIN support in version 3.39.0.

## Query behaviour, NULLs and dialect notes
The examples target SQLite. A join predicate that evaluates to TRUE forms a matching pair; FALSE or UNKNOWN does not. Outer joins create NULL-extended columns for missing partners. Use a non-nullable key from the missing side when detecting unmatched rows. Do not assume RIGHT or FULL OUTER JOIN exists in every engine version: SQLite supports them from 3.39.0; older SQLite releases can express a RIGHT JOIN by reversing inputs and using LEFT JOIN. For older engines without FULL OUTER JOIN, a carefully constructed UNION ALL of a left join and right-only rows may be required. Always verify the target DBMS documentation.

## Common mistakes
- Joining on the wrong columns or omitting part of a composite relationship.
- Forgetting that one-to-many matches produce multiple output rows.
- Using SELECT * in a report with repeated column names.
- Filtering a nullable right-side column in WHERE and unintentionally removing unmatched rows from a LEFT JOIN.
- Counting rows with COUNT(*) when COUNT(right_table.primary_key) is needed to count matches.

## Best practices
Use explicit JOIN syntax, meaningful aliases, qualified column names and a deliberate ON condition. Test with matched, unmatched and duplicate-match data. Add ORDER BY when output order matters; SQL does not guarantee row order without it.

